---
source: crates/sluice-cli/tests/help.rs
expression: "help(&[\"daemon\", \"--help\"])"
---
Serve the control interface on a socket or pipe only this user can reach, or ask the one that runs

The daemon runs transfers for local programs. It sends from and receives into the folders named with `--root` and nowhere else, and with none it refuses every send and receive. It holds the home's lock, so a command that opens the same home, `send` among them, is refused while it runs. `status` and `stop` call a daemon that runs.

Usage: sluice daemon [OPTIONS]
       sluice daemon <COMMAND>

Commands:
  status  Print the running daemon's version and its jobs
  stop    Stop the running daemon: its jobs end as cancelled, a receive keeps its partial store, and the socket or pipe is removed
  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --json
          Print JSON, one object per line, instead of text. A failure is then a JSON line on stderr

      --color <WHEN>
          When to colour help, errors and other output. NO_COLOR set to anything turns it off

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a stream only when it is a terminal
          - always: Colour always, unless `NO_COLOR` is set
          - never:  Never colour
          
          [default: auto]

      --root <DIR>
          A folder the daemon may send from and receive into, written as a client names it; repeat for more. Without one the daemon refuses every send and every receive

      --relay <URL>
          The relay that carries a transfer when a direct path fails

      --n0-relays
          Use n0's public relays and DNS discovery (not run by this project)

      --loopback
          Listen for transfers on 127.0.0.1 only, with no relay, no mDNS, no port mapping and no address publishing, so nothing outside this computer can reach them. It cannot be combined with `--relay` or `--n0-relays`

      --ui
          Also serve a page and the control interface as JSON over HTTP on 127.0.0.1, on a port the system picks, for `sluice ui`. A key made at start guards every call, and the port and key go to a file in the home that only this user can read

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
